1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,350
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:04,660
Episode 31.

3
00:00:04,660 --> 00:00:07,173
The River That Wrote in Rock.

4
00:00:07,173 --> 00:00:09,891
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:09,891 --> 00:00:15,475
Did the fast change in the sand tray prove that the Grand Canyon formed equally fast?

6
00:00:15,475 --> 00:00:16,902
Ready?

7
00:00:16,902 --> 00:00:18,980
Let us find out.

8
00:00:18,980 --> 00:00:22,500
Maya finds a photograph of the Grand Canyon.

9
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:27,866
Bands of rock stretch across enormous walls, and a river lies far below.

10
00:00:27,866 --> 00:00:29,728
Who dug that?

11
00:00:29,728 --> 00:00:31,671
Leo asks.

12
00:00:31,671 --> 00:00:34,456
They would need a very large shovel.

13
00:00:34,456 --> 00:00:38,464
Pip calculates how many toy shovels the club owns.

14
00:00:38,464 --> 00:00:41,019
The number is not promising.

15
00:00:41,019 --> 00:00:48,084
Ms. Park explains that the Colorado River and other processes shaped the canyon over a very long time.

16
00:00:48,084 --> 00:00:51,277
Flowing water carries material away.

17
00:00:51,277 --> 00:00:57,771
Weathering breaks rock down, and side streams and falling rock also help change the landscape.

18
00:00:57,771 --> 00:01:00,733
The club builds a small sand tray.

19
00:01:00,733 --> 00:01:05,624
With their teacher, they pour a gentle stream of water over one area.

20
00:01:05,624 --> 00:01:10,421
A channel begins to form, and sand moves toward the lower end.

21
00:01:10,421 --> 00:01:13,654
This happened quickly, Leo says.

22
00:01:13,654 --> 00:01:17,241
Did the real canyon form that quickly, too?

23
00:01:17,241 --> 00:01:18,722
No.

24
00:01:18,722 --> 00:01:22,907
Our tray shows an idea about moving water and material.

25
00:01:22,907 --> 00:01:28,097
Its scale, materials, and time are very different from the real canyon.

26
00:01:28,097 --> 00:01:31,630
Maya draws arrows showing where the sand traveled.

27
00:01:31,630 --> 00:01:36,562
Pip adds a sign beside the new channel: Landscape under construction.

28
00:01:36,562 --> 00:01:39,986
Later, they look again at the photograph.

29
00:01:39,986 --> 00:01:44,456
The rock layers remind Maya of pages in a very old book.

30
00:01:44,456 --> 00:01:46,603
Can we read all of it?

31
00:01:46,603 --> 00:01:48,423
she asks.

32
00:01:48,423 --> 00:01:53,002
Scientists are still studying its history, says Ms. Park.

33
00:01:53,002 --> 00:01:55,774
Leo puts away the toy shovel.

34
00:01:55,774 --> 00:02:01,073
He decides that patient questions are more useful than enormous digging plans.

35
00:02:01,073 --> 00:02:03,980
Now, a little more to discover.

36
00:02:03,980 --> 00:02:09,592
The Grand Canyon records a long history of rock formation and landscape change.

37
00:02:09,592 --> 00:02:18,111
The Colorado River cut downward while weathering, tributary streams, and other processes helped widen and shape the canyon.

38
00:02:18,111 --> 00:02:25,978
A classroom erosion model can illustrate one process without reproducing the real landscape's full history or timescale.

39
00:02:25,978 --> 00:02:28,817
Let us hear the main idea again.

40
00:02:28,817 --> 00:02:32,391
The photograph led the club to study erosion.

41
00:02:32,391 --> 00:02:37,472
Water moved sand in their tray, but the tray was only a simplified model.

42
00:02:37,472 --> 00:02:42,744
The real canyon was shaped by several processes over a much longer time.

43
00:02:42,744 --> 00:02:45,720
Here are three useful expressions.

44
00:02:45,720 --> 00:02:48,845
You can listen, or say them with me.

45
00:02:48,845 --> 00:02:51,562
It changed over a long time.

46
00:02:51,562 --> 00:02:54,279
It changed over a long time.

47
00:02:54,279 --> 00:02:56,725
Where did the material go?

48
00:02:56,725 --> 00:02:59,171
Where did the material go?

49
00:02:59,171 --> 00:03:01,793
Our model is much smaller.

50
00:03:01,793 --> 00:03:04,415
Our model is much smaller.

51
00:03:04,415 --> 00:03:07,010
Remember our listening question?

52
00:03:07,010 --> 00:03:12,595
Did the fast change in the sand tray prove that the Grand Canyon formed equally fast?

53
00:03:12,595 --> 00:03:14,728
Here is the answer.

54
00:03:14,728 --> 00:03:16,209
No.

55
00:03:16,209 --> 00:03:21,005
The model used different materials, scale, and conditions.

56
00:03:21,005 --> 00:03:25,896
It illustrated a process, not the real canyon's full timescale.

57
00:03:25,896 --> 00:03:29,551
And here is a question just for your imagination.

58
00:03:29,551 --> 00:03:34,538
Where can you notice small signs of water moving soil or sand after rain?

59
00:03:34,538 --> 00:03:37,649
There is more than one possible answer.

60
00:03:37,649 --> 00:03:40,896
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
